{"title":"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 8","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Side_Note_(Making_Things_a_Little_Easier)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Side Note (Making Things a Little Easier)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Guess_&amp;_Check)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Guess &amp; Check)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_MadMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by MadMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Isosceles triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/4/5f4c25cb7b6078137512af0317bd262160bd0319.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=BC$\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> be the incenter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />. The perimeters of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/a/cfaf358547caaa16b58fe2bd36f435aa30734f97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AIC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> are in the ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/c/f1c606a18884825538075ca692ab11c2cffd6b03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125:6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" />, and all the sides of both triangles have integer lengths. Find the minimum possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/1/ba1fa15a30620633a0298924db21e9fec43e6d81.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(200,0);  pair A=(-5,0); pair C=(5,0); pair D=(0,0); pair B=(0,12); pair I=(0,3);  dot(A,red); dot(B,red); dot(C,red); dot(D,red); dot(I,red);  label(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,SW); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,N); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,SE); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,S); label(&quot;$I$&quot;,I,W);  draw(A--B); draw(B--C); draw(A--C); draw(B--D); draw(A--I); draw(C--I);  draw((0,0.4)--(0.4,0.4)--(0.4,0)); draw((0,0.4)--(-0.4,0.4)--(-0.4,0));  label(&quot;$1$&quot;,(A+D)/2,S); label(&quot;$1$&quot;,(D+C)/2,S); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(D+I)/2,W); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(D+I)/2,E); label(&quot;$\\sqrt{1+x^2}$&quot;,(A+I)/2,N); label(&quot;$\\sqrt{1+x^2}$&quot;,(C+I)/2,N); label(&quot;$\\frac{1+x^2}{1-x^2}$&quot;,(A+B)/2,W); label(&quot;$\\frac{1+x^2}{1-x^2}$&quot;,(B+C)/2,E); label(&quot;$\\theta$&quot;,4(A+D)/5,NE); label(&quot;$\\theta$&quot;,4(C+D)/5,NW); label(&quot;$\\theta$&quot;, (-4.3, 1.6), SE); label(&quot;$\\theta$&quot;, (4.3, 1.6), SW); [/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"395\" />\n</p>\n<div style=\"line-height:2;\">\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is isosceles with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/7/fb71d5ba363a314c0343efdd9a19aafe8070c75e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = BC$\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" />, the angle bisector, median, and altitude from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> coincide, which is the perpendicular bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. The incenter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> lies on this common line. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> be the intersection of this line with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/6/4568b78343b0ef65f5b89bc497e6619a6695ef16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ID \\perp AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"79\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>We set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/6/286e989082359d570543d0269004ce484008ce68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC = 2$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/1/6d16efd933755d348836344fd37ad7f396d5f7d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD = DC = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"118\" height=\"14\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/f/bcf7f289f9166f2c01e5ecc11243ff15da5c1d64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ID = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>The sides \\(AI\\) and \\(CI\\) are equal, by Pythagorean Theorem,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/7/7b7d43606ca1c8467785164a66a3ae6aa3dff988.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AI = CI = \\sqrt{AD^2 + ID^2} = \\sqrt{1 + x^2}.\\]\" width=\"293\" height=\"18\" />\nThus the perimeter of \\(\\triangle AIC\\) is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/9/a39d0f5e237bbc0423f6f417bdc1b9daf4db2f17.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Perimeter}(\\triangle AIC) = AC + AI + CI = 2 + 2\\sqrt{1 + x^2}.\\]\" width=\"414\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Let \\(\\angle IAD = \\theta\\). Then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/7/df78fc5dbee500f4fc6972abc1e4083c6abaa155.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\cos\\theta = \\frac{AD}{AI} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}}.\\]\" width=\"189\" height=\"42\" />\nUsing the double-angle formula for cosine:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/f/14f1b85baed7062e9962e18ef68d50c4954fa5d3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\cos 2\\theta = 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1 = 2\\left(\\frac{1}{1 + x^2}\\right) - 1 = \\frac{2 - (1 + x^2)}{1 + x^2} = \\frac{1 - x^2}{1 + x^2}.\\]\" width=\"507\" height=\"43\" />\nIn \\(\\triangle ABC\\), \\(\\angle BAC = 2\\theta\\), and \\(AD = 1\\) is half of \\(AC\\). Thus:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/1/b91b1043aa821035effeb6aa434118bbdac45c63.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AB = \\frac{AD}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{1 - x^2}{1 + x^2}} = \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 - x^2}.\\]\" width=\"245\" height=\"48\" />\nSince \\(AB = BC\\), the perimeter of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/3/38358415d37e8b39fe3b09ac3693af1528e72f27.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Perimeter}(\\triangle ABC) = AB + BC + AC = 2AB + 2 = 2\\left(\\frac{1 + x^2}{1 - x^2}\\right) + 2.\\]\" width=\"538\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>The ratio of perimeters is given as \\(125:6\\):\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/d/a1d297c1e35a98e4685de5f4d433d21e8da6629e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2\\left(\\frac{1 + x^2}{1 - x^2}\\right) + 2}{2 + 2\\sqrt{1 + x^2}} = \\frac{125}{6}.\\]\" width=\"165\" height=\"56\" />\nWe simplify the left-hand side:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/3/2b3838d96199cd607bc66411dc67f453efd32e8c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2\\left(\\frac{1 + x^2 + 1 - x^2}{1 - x^2}\\right)}{2(1 + \\sqrt{1 + x^2})} = \\frac{\\frac{2}{1 - x^2}}{1 + \\sqrt{1 + x^2}} = \\frac{2}{(1 - x^2)(1 + \\sqrt{1 + x^2})}.\\]\" width=\"449\" height=\"58\" />\nThus:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/a/99a9fdc9589cb9d14de95305d0cee3abf2ae8889.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2}{(1 - x^2)(1 + \\sqrt{1 + x^2})} = \\frac{125}{6}.\\]\" width=\"234\" height=\"44\" />\nCross-multiplying gives:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/c/f5cf6642c3879afc36e01810fbc4b406b31f7f85.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1 - x^2)(1 + \\sqrt{1 + x^2}) = \\frac{12}{125}.\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>We make a substitution, let \\(\\sqrt{1 + x^2} = \\dfrac{a}{5}\\) for some positive integer \\(a\\). Then:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/e/3eef4a90901a74a82e9c11623549860c51ac348b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 + x^2 = \\frac{a^2}{25} \\implies x^2 = \\frac{a^2 - 25}{25},\\]\" width=\"251\" height=\"39\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/6/ff629062cc5bc23abdf39e858b79112ddd7478d0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 - x^2 = 1 - \\frac{a^2 - 25}{25} = \\frac{25 - a^2 + 25}{25} = \\frac{50 - a^2}{25},\\]\" width=\"380\" height=\"39\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/5/405cf6db73f69dbf50ec0f918473c7fa4d659f8b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 + \\sqrt{1 + x^2} = 1 + \\frac{a}{5} = \\frac{5 + a}{5}.\\]\" width=\"236\" height=\"38\" />\nWe substitute into the equation:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/b/ccbaa08b0e7d949e7972dfb1adab08af1dac9093.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{50 - a^2}{25} \\cdot \\frac{5 + a}{5} = \\frac{12}{125}.\\]\" width=\"176\" height=\"39\" />\nSimplifying gives:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/f/cbf679daa0240f56a85c0ddb6101e2fd469f3922.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{(50 - a^2)(5 + a)}{125} = \\frac{12}{125} \\implies (50 - a^2)(5 + a) = 12.\\]\" width=\"406\" height=\"39\" />\nWe guess and check small positive integer values for \\(a\\), and find \\(a = 7\\) satisfies the equation:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/0/910fb131c13a2f371c92a14a39f2e9b1b269e5c1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(50 - 7^2)(5 + 7) = (50 - 49)(12) = 1 \\cdot 12 = 12.\\]\" width=\"363\" height=\"20\" />\nThus \\(a = 7\\), so:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/d/63df04e4b32950e71a9336512c46860ea5a18746.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{1 + x^2} = \\frac{7}{5} \\implies 1 + x^2 = \\frac{49}{25} \\implies x^2 = \\frac{24}{25}.\\]\" width=\"363\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>From \\(AB = \\dfrac{1 + x^2}{1 - x^2}\\):\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/b/ebb110a5736231268fa3adbae296b29d5148617a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1 - x^2 = 1 - \\frac{24}{25} = \\frac{1}{25}, \\quad AB = \\frac{\\frac{49}{25}}{\\frac{1}{25}} = 49.\\]\" width=\"316\" height=\"47\" />\nIn our initial setup, \\(AC = 2\\) and \\(AI = \\dfrac{7}{5}\\). To make all sides integers, we scale by a factor of \\(5\\), so \\(AC = 10\\), \\(AI = 7\\), and:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/3/a939329f74377588af1c6f7a58a6a7a3ed1eaeb3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AB_{\\text{min}} = 49 \\times 5 = \\boxed{245}.\\]\" width=\"190\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n</div>\n<p>~Steven Zheng\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is isosceles with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/7/fb71d5ba363a314c0343efdd9a19aafe8070c75e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = BC$\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" />, the angle bisector and altitude from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> coincide, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/7/6479e1655e7bf7b4c45d73e0d2086f0d4aa8694e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BI \\perp AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"78\" height=\"14\" />. Letting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/0/be0834a58059a61a79d86701cc1292ee73a0858c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC=b$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/7/ac780589ab432500f90cabe77394d7b249c5ce12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = \\frac{b}{2\\cos A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"112\" height=\"39\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/6/cd6ec62f6a8b84041e7c683d4538dcf162eb0531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AI=\\frac{b}{2\\cos \\frac{A}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"107\" height=\"46\" />. Substituting into perimeter, and letting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/3/3e3eccd33f4020777b700b232d9de5bab8c845a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos \\frac{A}{2}=x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"39\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/4/144afb258ef305b0bf323953c561cfaa0cb95c2d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dfrac{2AB+b}{2AI+b} = \\dfrac{\\frac{b}{\\cos A}+b}{\\frac{b}{\\cos \\frac{A}{2}}+b}=\\dfrac{\\frac{1}{2x^2-1}+1}{\\frac{1}{x}+1}=\\dfrac{125}{6}\\]\" width=\"319\" height=\"53\" /> Expanding out that last in equality, we find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/d/c3d4dd56902eda29e96c879c660eb5792498b7d9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[238x^3+250x^2-125x-125=0\\]\" width=\"254\" height=\"17\" /> Fortunately, we can make the substitutions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/591c2ae4d3ad69acaf6e2d2adbfdb63be6515067.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 5y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"17\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/9/b795f3e053908f1cb98ebb974c68077f3a101f72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = \\frac{1}{z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"49\" height=\"38\" />, which simplifies the equations into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/b/48b2c54c078c206ba0d50f9e258f4f44e2774fdb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[238x^3+250x^2-125x-125=0 \\implies 238y^3+50y^2-5y-1=0 \\implies z^3+5z^2-50z-238=0\\]\" width=\"684\" height=\"19\" /> Much nicer. Checking via rational root theorem, we find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/a/04a297f4a650b7dd42cc64198204345fbc873164.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=7$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> is one such solution, and there are no other rational solutions. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/9/e7910c9bcace6989dfb0db54e9a0feecb6d4fa98.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\cos \\frac{A}{2}= x = 5y = \\frac{5}{z} = \\frac{5}{7}\\text{, and } \\cos A = 2x^2-1 = \\frac{1}{49}\\]\" width=\"410\" height=\"37\" /> Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/023872f6952679c2e4a3f518118801dcee8a2109.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2AB = 49b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"89\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/c/6ace3ae13a413da846f3d884a2d659c44ec44e61.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10AI = 7b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"84\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/4/354f2d348c277752e0fabfbd8b69cb4629d4f793.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10 \\mid b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"43\" height=\"20\" />. The smallest value for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> is 10. Thus, the minimum value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/8/7a80d47ee6d5303053a44ab398646b5f0dc06347.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{49\\cdot 10}{2}=\\boxed{245}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"118\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>~sillybone\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Side_Note_.28Making_Things_a_Little_Easier.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Side_Note_(Making_Things_a_Little_Easier)\">Side Note (Making Things a Little Easier)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Side Note (Making Things a Little Easier)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Consider the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/5/e95409021e424ee83546a071dfb489895073537a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$238x^3+250x^2-125x-125=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"257\" height=\"18\" />  It is cubic and has no apparent real root.\n</p><p>According to Vieta's Theorem, the numerator of a real root of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> should be a factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b829a6f0810a25e9ca9f85d690f2bafd0f2c1773.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, and the denominator should be a factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/e/6be3566092a59b4feffa5da5647d0fe3a651b968.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$238$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/1/261f025b4f04e207892d1a8854a524fc2f068304.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;\\frac{A}{2}&lt;\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"92\" height=\"39\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/8/f38f1c3c982b8c837a2643175c692ec23b4c82a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}&lt;\\cos{\\frac{A}{2}}&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"133\" height=\"43\" />, there are only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> possible values: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/5/4b5d470235923cdb77a71e1cd433e35b139a4713.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/a/58ad53036b621153cf9c927627ef99320f2b8ae1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{25}{34}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" />. Plug in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/5/4b5d470235923cdb77a71e1cd433e35b139a4713.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> to make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/9/b397bec31399084533508de03c687b2391bf23b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos{A} = 2\\cos^{2}{\\frac{A}{2}} - 1 = \\frac{1}{49}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"209\" height=\"39\" />. We get lengths of the sides of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> iscoceles, letting half of the base to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> —— <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34c8e6bf4d57cd6f2bb0d7d43e00f0f442f53b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$49$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/4/764672badb627a33194f14120604195d46988f8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{7}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />, which fits the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/c/f1c606a18884825538075ca692ab11c2cffd6b03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125:6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> ratio.\n</p><p>We don't need a rigorous proof for why <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/a/58ad53036b621153cf9c927627ef99320f2b8ae1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{25}{34}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"38\" /> is unsuitable in this situation: Even if it is an actual root of the equation, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/3/4c3f55016e780597e0d9bcfc74f5ac6917e1754b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos{A} = \\frac{2\\cdot25^2-34^2}{34^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"167\" height=\"41\" />, a value with a large, complicated numerator. It would result in a much larger minimum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/5/4b5d470235923cdb77a71e1cd433e35b139a4713.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>~cassphe\n</p><p>Note that we must have some rational root, since we need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/4/d54938fcc32103ea6771b35f37cb97064198c16e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{AB}{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"31\" height=\"38\" /> to be rational. Also, the motivation for substitution is to simplify the cubic, so Rational Root theorem is simpler. Motivation for me was that I can calculate cubics faster for integers inputs, rather than fractions.\n</p><p>~sillybone\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Guess_.26_Check.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Guess_&amp;_Check)\">Solution 3 (Guess &amp; Check)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Guess &amp; Check)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Clearly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> must make up most of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />'s perimeter. We can set the perimeter of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/7/a47df20e00b420d04fa4a7beb285b193ccaf6e29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125x,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" /> and the perimeter of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/a/cfaf358547caaa16b58fe2bd36f435aa30734f97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AIC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/1/d11ac493971db95a31fc14390234837d30226f80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6x.$\" width=\"23\" height=\"12\" /> We do not need to check many values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/0/6d0382f3585219947b495ac2262a81b7b0c40db2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> because if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> was too large then side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> would be greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/4/6b434ebc2071e706df740975a3c90aad3cfa1455.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"13\" /> After checking values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/0/6d0382f3585219947b495ac2262a81b7b0c40db2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> we can find that the value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> works if side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/d/66d3517b4373dec132cc1af0009a90406d5a32f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$245,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"17\" /> side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/7/517151f6f181bc7bcbfe164ac5c211c84abc0e92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"16\" /> and side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/d/6ed34bab93e79a30ad406a057535bcca41dab308.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IA=7.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" /> We can double check this by finding the inradius of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> using the formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/9/76934cc74e2bb780cfd5414a0b0a62b6d8235a5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{A}{s},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"25\" height=\"39\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is the area and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is the semiperimeter. Using Heron's formula for the area, we can find that the inradius is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/d/75d8109c64a70f3cb47d852c8fa91c69fd0fed6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{24},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"23\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/6/8f60fd9de19041a9835629af2280d8e491773cb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\sqrt 6.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"40\" height=\"21\" /> If we draw the lines connecting the incircle to the sides of the triangle, we know that they form right angles with the sides from the tangent circle theorem. Using the Pythagorean Theorem on these sides, we can see that the side lengths do in fact work, giving us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/d/7fd8356270caf7d505f83d70dfdd815d38a6da2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=\\boxed {245}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"93\" height=\"25\" /> as our answer.\n</p><p>~Nodskin Goble\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution 1 by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>With Discussion about various problem solving techniques. Least mentally taxing solution process: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ivK4vL-ovCY\">https://youtu.be/ivK4vL-ovCY</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_MadMath\">Video Solution 2 by MadMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by MadMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/rwvUzpf78aw\">https://youtu.be/rwvUzpf78aw</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II\" title=\"2026 AIME II\">2026 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Answer_Key\" title=\"2026 AIME II Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2026\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 7\">Problem 7</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 9\">Problem 9</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\